.TH PGREP 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pgrep \- look up processes by name
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pgrep
.RB [ \-l ]
.RB [ \-x ]
.RB [ \-u
.IR uid ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.I pattern
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pgrep
takes a snapshot of the process table and writes, one per line, the
process ID of every process whose command name contains
.I pattern
as a substring.
.I pattern
is a plain string, not a regular expression, and the comparison is
case-sensitive.
Only the process name is examined, not its arguments.
.PP
Process ID 0 is never reported.
The
.B pgrep
process itself is included if its name matches.
There is no
.B pkill
counterpart.
.SH OPTIONS
Each option must be given as a separate argument.
.TP
.B \-l
Print the process name after each process ID, separated by a space.
.TP
.B \-x
Require
.I pattern
to match the whole process name exactly.
.TP
.BI \-u " uid"
Only consider processes whose real user ID is
.IR uid .
.I uid
must be a non-negative decimal number; user names are not accepted.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one process matched.
.TP
.B 1
No process matched, or the process table could not be read.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage, a missing
.IR pattern ,
or memory exhaustion.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
$ pgrep \-l sh
12 sh
40 zsh
$ pgrep \-x init || echo "init not running"
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ps (1),
.BR top (1),
.BR proc (5)
